## Summary

A whirlpool spinner (`static/js/spinner.js`, canvas-based, animated via `requestAnimationFrame`) can run its rAF loop forever when its element is never appended to the DOM (or is hidden), burning CPU and churning canvas allocations continuously for something nobody can see. Measured live: `Spinner._drawWhirlpool` scheduling ~43 rAF callbacks/second while idle, with zero canvas elements in the DOM — an orphaned spinner looping on a detached canvas. In a long session this is a dominant continuous producer and saturates the renderer main thread (a CPU cost as well as memory churn).

## Root cause

`createWhirlpool()` / `createLoadingRow()` build the spinner, call `sp.start()` (rAF loop begins immediately), and return a detached element for the caller to append. The self-terminate guard:

```js
const connected = !!(this.element && this.element.isConnected);
if (connected) this._wpWasConnected = true;
if (connected || !this._wpWasConnected) {
  this.rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => this._drawWhirlpool());
} else { this.isRunning = false; }
```

`!this._wpWasConnected` is an unbounded grace period. If the caller never appends the element (fast/synchronous load replaces the container before the next frame, append to a detached subtree, or an early return), `_wpWasConnected` stays false forever and the loop never ends. Separately, `isConnected` is true for `display:none` elements, so a spinner inside a closed-but-resident panel also never stops.

## Fix

Bound the grace window and gate on visibility, not mere connectedness: terminate if the spinner was never made visible (`isConnected && offsetParent !== null`) within ~2s, or if it was visible and then hidden/removed. Stops orphaned and hidden spinners; leaves genuinely-visible loading spinners running.

## Problem

Moving the mouse over any element in the primary UI layout (sidebar icons, chat input, section headers, tabs) grows RSS from ~320 MB to ~607 MB+ without recovery. Three causes in `static/style.css`:

### 1. `email-notif-breathe` animates `box-shadow` at 60 fps

`box-shadow` is not compositor-promoted. Every frame of the infinite `2.2s ease-in-out` loop requires main-thread rasterization and deposits a raster tile. Qt's embedded renderer receives no OS memory pressure signals, so `cc::TileManager` never evicts these tiles. Growth is continuous and unbounded for as long as the app is open.

### 2. 77 occurrences of `transition: all`

`transition: all 0.15s` on icon rail buttons, input buttons, section headers, tabs, and other primary interactive elements transitions every CSS property on hover — including non-compositor-promoted ones (background-color, border-color, color). Each hover entry/exit cycle deposits ~9 raster tile frames at 60fps per property per element. At normal interaction speed the accumulation rivals idle animation.

### 3. No CSS containment on major layout regions

Without containment boundaries, a repaint triggered by hover propagates up through all ancestors before being bounded by the viewport. Adds CPU cost to every hover event.

## Fix

**Phase 2B — `contain: layout paint style` on major structural containers:**

```css
.sidebar          { contain: layout paint style; }
#chat-container   { contain: layout paint style; }
.chat-input-bar   { contain: layout paint style; }
```

**Phase 3A — Replace `email-notif-breathe` box-shadow with opacity:**

```css
@keyframes email-notif-breathe {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 0.7; }
  50%       { opacity: 1; }
}
/* Remove box-shadow from keyframe and base rule */
```

**Phase 3B — Replace `transition: all` with specific compositor-safe properties** on `.icon-rail-btn`, `.input-icon-btn`, `.section-header-btn`, `.tab-btn`, `.tab-btn-sm`:

```css
.icon-rail-btn {
  transition: transform 0.15s, opacity 0.15s;  /* was: transition: all 0.15s */
}
```

Visual effect is preserved — these elements use transform/opacity for their primary hover feedback. Color and background transitions are imperceptible on top of icon-based feedback.

## Scope

- `static/style.css`
- Branch: `fix/css-hover-raster-global` (from `upstream-mirror`)

## Research

See `docs/fork/memory-explosion-research.md` Session 9 — Root causes 2, 3, 4 and Phases 2B, 3.

## Summary

`tool_start` starts two intervals on `currentToolBubble`: `_waveInterval` (100 ms) and `_elapsedTicker` (50 ms). The only cleanup point is `tool_output`, but `tool_output` opens with `if (_isBg) continue;` — a background session switch skips the rest of the handler, leaving both intervals running against a detached node indefinitely.

A second related bug: `_evictLive` can remove the node `currentToolBubble` points to before `tool_output` arrives. Subsequent writes to the detached node silently fail.

## Proposed Fix

In `static/js/chat.js`, move timer cleanup unconditionally before the `_isBg` guard, and add an `isConnected` guard after it:

```js
} else if (json.type === 'tool_output') {
  // Timer cleanup runs unconditionally — tool_start always starts these
  // and this is the only reliable cleanup point; the background path
  // skips the rest of this handler.
  if (currentToolBubble) {
    if (currentToolBubble._waveInterval) {
      clearInterval(currentToolBubble._waveInterval);
      currentToolBubble._waveInterval = null;
    }
    if (currentToolBubble._elapsedTicker) {
      clearInterval(currentToolBubble._elapsedTicker);
      currentToolBubble._elapsedTicker = null;
    }
  }
  if (_isBg) continue;
  if (!currentToolBubble || !currentToolBubble.isConnected) {
    console.warn('[chatHistory] tool_output: bubble evicted before completion');
    continue;
  }
  // ... rest of handler
```

## Impact

Leaking intervals keep JS alive and prevent GC of the bubble node (holds DOM ref + closures over header/wave/content element refs). In long agentic sessions with many tool calls, this compounds the RSS growth documented in the OOM investigation. The `isConnected` guard prevents silent data loss when a bubble is evicted by `_evictLive` mid-flight.

## Context

Identified during the senior review pass of the Oilpan OOM investigation. Research doc: `docs/fork/memory-explosion-research.md`.

## Problem

Tool call bubbles in agent sessions go through two full innerHTML replacements:
1. At tool start: `node.innerHTML = initialHtml` (creates running state with wave spinner)
2. At tool end: `node.innerHTML = finalHtml` (replaces with completed state)

Each replacement detaches the previous DOM subtree and creates a new one. For an agent session with 50 tool calls, this is 50 complete detached subtrees per session.

## Fix

Track key child element references on the node at creation time, then patch only what changed at completion:

```js
// Tool start — create once, store refs:
node.innerHTML = initialHtml;
node._toolIcon    = node.querySelector('.agent-thread-icon');
node._toolStatus  = node.querySelector('.agent-thread-status');
node._toolContent = node.querySelector('.agent-thread-content');
node._toolWave    = node.querySelector('.agent-thread-wave');

// Tool end — patch only changed elements:
if (node._toolIcon)    node._toolIcon.textContent   = ok ? '✓' : '✗';
if (node._toolStatus)  node._toolStatus.textContent  = ok ? 'done' : 'failed';
if (node._toolWave)    node._toolWave.remove();
if (node._toolContent) node._toolContent.innerHTML   = cmdHtml + outHtml + diffHtml;
```

The `.agent-thread-content` (output area) still uses innerHTML because its content is
generated HTML — only the header/status elements are safely patchable as text.

## Classification

Upstream-candidate.

## Problem

After a streaming response completes (`[DONE]`), `chat.js` always re-renders the response body via `_body4.innerHTML = processWithThinking(squashOutsideCode(finalDisplay))`. This discards everything the StreamRenderer built incrementally — one complete response worth of DOM nodes all detached simultaneously. This is the largest single-event Oilpan deposition per response.

For responses with no thinking blocks and no sources/findings, the `finalDisplay` text IS already what the StreamRenderer rendered — there is no post-processing needed. The re-render is pure waste.

## Fix

Detect when no transformation is needed and call `streamingRenderer.finalize()` instead:

```js
var _hasThinking = _extracted && _extracted.thinkingBlocks && _extracted.thinkingBlocks.length;
var _hasSources  = !!(_sourcesData || _findingsData || _sourcesHtml);

if (!_hasThinking && !_hasSources && !_liveReplyEl) {
  var _sc = roundHolder.querySelector('.stream-content');
  if (_sc && _sc._streamRenderer) {
    _sc._streamRenderer.finalize();
    _sc._streamRenderer = null;
  }
} else {
  // existing thinking/sources/full-rerender logic unchanged
}
```

Thinking responses and responses with sources still go through the existing path unchanged.

## Impact

Eliminates the largest per-response Oilpan deposition event for plain prose responses. Most non-agent responses (direct chat, no tool output) qualify for the fast path.

## Classification

Upstream-candidate.

## Problem

`renderTail()` in `streamingRenderer.js` is called on every SSE token. Even with the in-place fast-path patch, every call creates a holder div, sets its innerHTML (full markdown parse → full DOM subtree), reads `childNodes`, and then discards the holder+subtree if the fast path matched. At 200 tok/sec this is 200 holder divs and 200 full DOM subtrees created and discarded per second in Oilpan.

Capping at requestAnimationFrame cadence (~60fps) reduces these allocations 3× at high token rates with no visible rendering degradation — the display already updates at most once per frame.

## Fix

In `chat.js`, wrap the per-SSE-token `_renderStream()` call with a rAF guard:

```js
// module-level
let _streamRenderRaf = null;

// in the SSE token handler, replace direct _renderStream():
if (!_streamRenderRaf) {
  _streamRenderRaf = requestAnimationFrame(function () {
    _streamRenderRaf = null;
    _renderStream();
  });
}
```

The existing StreamRenderer state (`lastText`) always holds the most recent full text, so the rAF callback renders the latest token even if intermediate tokens were skipped.

## Depends on

Issue #XX (renderTail call counter) — fast-path ratio data should be collected first to confirm this optimization is worthwhile.

## Classification

Upstream-candidate.

## Problem

After each streaming response, `chat.js` fires `gc({ type: 'major', execution: 'async' })` with a 2.5s delay. Async GC runs incrementally across multiple frames — the full cycle may take 3–6 seconds. If the user sends another message before the previous GC cycle finishes, a second `gc()` call is dispatched while the first is still running its incremental slices. Stacked concurrent cycles double GC overhead without extra collection benefit and may increase per-frame pause time.

## Fix

Add a module-level `_gcPending` flag in `chat.js`:

```js
let _gcPending = false;

setTimeout(function () {
  if (typeof gc === 'function' && !_gcPending) {
    _gcPending = true;
    gc({ type: 'major', execution: 'async' });
    setTimeout(function () { _gcPending = false; }, 5000);
  } else if (!_gcPending && typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined') {
    requestIdleCallback(function () {}, { timeout: 2000 });
  }
}, 2500);
```

The 5-second reset window is conservative — incremental major GC on a 200 MB heap typically completes in under 3 seconds spread across idle slices.

## Classification

Upstream-candidate. Pure GC coordination improvement.

## Problem

`hljs.highlightElement()` is called immediately after every message render, including for code blocks that are entirely off-screen. Each highlighted code block allocates 200–500 `<span>` elements in Oilpan (Blink's GC). In long agent sessions with many tool-output code blocks, this generates thousands of span elements that are never seen by the user before they scroll off and are evicted.

**Observed impact**: During active agent sessions, a significant fraction of Oilpan's detached-node count comes from hljs spans allocated for code blocks the user never scrolled to.

## Root cause

All `highlightElement()` calls fire synchronously at finalization time, even for:
- Code blocks in messages loaded from session history (Phase 1 window load)
- Code blocks that are scrolled above the current viewport
- Tool output blocks in long agent threads that are off-screen

## Fix

Introduce `hljsDefer.js` — a single `IntersectionObserver` that queues `pre code` elements and calls `hljs.highlightElement()` only when a block scrolls within 200 px of the viewport.

**Deferred callsites** (off-screen content, high volume):
- `streamingRenderer.js` `freeze()` — committed streaming blocks (may be above viewport)
- `chat.js` — agent round finalization (`roundHolder.querySelectorAll`)
- `chatRenderer.js` — `addMessage()` history-load render

**Kept immediate** (user is actively viewing the result):
- Mid-stream stop handler
- Continuation stop handler
- Variant switch
- Research result render

## Implementation

```js
// hljsDefer.js
const _obs = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
    const block = entry.target;
    _obs.unobserve(block);
    if (window.hljs && !block.classList.contains('hljs')) {
      window.hljs.highlightElement(block);
    }
  }
}, { rootMargin: '200px 0px' });

export function deferHighlight(block) { ... }
export function deferHighlightAll(root) { ... }
```

`streamingRenderer.js` `freeze()` is restructured to insert nodes into the live DOM first, then observe — so `IntersectionObserver` can measure viewport distance on live nodes rather than a detached holder.

`fullRender()` (degraded fallback path) retains immediate highlight for simplicity.

## Effect

Code blocks that are near or in the viewport are highlighted within one observer tick (~16 ms) — no perceptible delay. Blocks that are off-screen are highlighted on demand when the user scrolls to them. For a tool-heavy agent session with 50+ tool calls, 2 code blocks each, this defers highlighting for ~90% of blocks that are scrolled above the current viewport.

## Bug

When large content arrives in the chat — a code block, an image, or a long markdown response — auto-scroll stops following the bottom and the user has to scroll down manually.

## Root Cause

`_smoothScrollStep()` in `static/js/ui.js` guards against forcing the user back to the bottom if they have intentionally scrolled up:

```javascript
// If user scrolled up significantly, don't force them down
if (diff > 300) {
  _scrollRafId = null;
  return;
}
```

`diff` is `scrollHeight - clientHeight - scrollTop` (distance from current position to bottom). When large content arrives, `scrollHeight` can jump by several hundred pixels in a single animation frame. If the user was near the bottom, this pushes `diff` past 300px and the guard fires — even though the user never scrolled up.

## Fix

Replace the rigid 300px threshold with a viewport-scaled value:

```javascript
const viewportHeight = box.clientHeight;
const maxAllowedDiff = Math.max(300, viewportHeight * 1.5);
if (diff > maxAllowedDiff) {
  _scrollRafId = null;
  return;
}
```

The 300px floor keeps the guard active on small viewports. On a typical 800px desktop viewport the threshold becomes 1200px, which tolerates any realistic content-driven layout shift while still suppressing auto-scroll when the user has genuinely scrolled far up.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Open a chat session with a model that produces code blocks or images.
2. Send a message that generates a long code response.
3. Observe: chat stops following the bottom mid-response; code block is not visible without manual scroll.

## Environment

Any platform. Not introduced by fork-specific changes — present in upstream `static/js/ui.js`.

### Problem

\`_load_stored_hf_token()\` in \`routes/cookbook_routes.py\` only reads from \`data/cookbook_state.json\`. It returns \`""\` if no token has been stored via the Cookbook UI.

Users who set \`HF_TOKEN\` in their shell environment (common on Linux dev machines, Docker setups, and CI) get \`[odysseus] HF token: NOT SET\` in the log even though the env var is present and the download subprocess inherits it correctly. The Python-side fallback never checks the environment, so \`_validate_token()\` runs on an empty string and the download script is generated without an explicit \`export HF_TOKEN=\` line — the subprocess inherits the var, so downloads still work, but the warning is misleading and the explicit export is missing.

### Root Cause

\`\`\`python
def _load_stored_hf_token() -> str:
    if not _cookbook_state_path.exists():
        return ""
    try:
        state = json.loads(_cookbook_state_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        env = state.get("env") if isinstance(state, dict) else {}
        return _decrypt_secret(env.get("hfToken") if isinstance(env, dict) else "")
    except Exception:
        return ""
\`\`\`

No check for \`os.environ.get("HF_TOKEN")\`.

### Fix

Prefer the stored (encrypted) token; fall back to \`HF_TOKEN\` / \`HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN\` env vars:

\`\`\`python
def _load_stored_hf_token() -> str:
    if _cookbook_state_path.exists():
        try:
            state = json.loads(_cookbook_state_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            env = state.get("env") if isinstance(state, dict) else {}
            stored = _decrypt_secret(env.get("hfToken") if isinstance(env, dict) else "")
            if stored:
                return stored
        except Exception:
            pass
    return os.environ.get("HF_TOKEN") or os.environ.get("HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN") or ""
\`\`\`

### Related

- Upstream #3829 / PR #3864 — fix the hint message that pointed to the wrong UI location (separate issue)
- Upstream #3422 / PR #3459 — fixed duplicate handler that cleared the token in the Cookbook UI (already merged)
- Fork #7 — HF token persistence (this is the remaining gap after #3459)

### Branch

\`fix/hf-token-env-fallback\` (from \`upstream-mirror\`)
